WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.856
[MUSICAL INTRO]

00:00:09.515 --> 00:00:10.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.560
SPEAKER: Every cell in our body
contains a copy of our genome,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.960
over 20,000 genes, 3
billion letters of DNA.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.650
DNA consists of two strands,
twisted into a double helix

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.790
and held together by
a simple pairing rule.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:31.320
A pairs with T, and G
pairs with C. Our genes

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.880
shape who we are as
individuals and as a species.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.580
Genes also have profound
effects on health,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.730
and thanks to advances
in DNA sequencing,

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.280
researchers have identified
thousands of genes that

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.220
affect our risk of disease.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.740
To understand how
genes work, researchers

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.500
need ways to control them.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.470
Changing genes in living
cells is not easy,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.720
but recently a new
method has been developed

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.730
that promises to
dramatically improve

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.560
our ability to edit the DNA of
any species, including humans.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:11.180
The CRISPR method is based on a
natural system used by bacteria

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.656
to protect themselves
from infection by viruses.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.250
When the bacterium detects
the presence of virus DNA,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.390
it produces two types of
short RNA, one of which

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.480
contains a sequence that matches
that of the invading virus.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.950
These two RNAs form a complex
with a protein called Cas9.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.555
Cas9 is a nuclease, a type
of enzyme that can cut DNA.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.810
When the matching sequence,
known as a guide RNA,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.100
finds its target within
the viral genome,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.275
the Cas9 cuts the target
DNA, disabling the virus.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.270
Over the past few years,
researchers studying the system

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.740
realize that it
could be engineered

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.850
to cut not just viral DNA but
any DNA sequence at a precisely

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.650
chosen location by changing the
guide RNA to match the target.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.800
And this can be done
not just in a test tube,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.190
but also within the
nucleus of a living cell.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.650
Once inside the nucleus,
the resulting complex

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:18.485
will lock onto a short
sequence known as the PAM.

00:02:18.485 --> 00:02:25.530
The Cas9 will unzip the DNA
and match it to its target RNA.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.930
If the match is
complete, the Cas9

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.600
will use two tiny molecular
scissors to cut the DNA.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.540
When this happens, the cell
tries to repair the cut,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.850
but the repair process
is error prone,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.460
leading to mutations that
can disable the gene,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.590
allowing researchers to
understand its function.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.890
These mutations are random,
but sometimes researchers

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.170
need to be more
precise, for example,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.280
by replacing a mutant
gene with a healthy copy.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.830
This can be done by adding
another piece of DNA that

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.160
carries the desired sequence.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.410
Once the CRISPR
system has made a cut,

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:08.170
this DNA template can
pair up with the cut ends,

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.750
recombining and replacing
the original sequence

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.980
with the new version.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.110
All this can be done
in cultured cells,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.240
including stem cells
that can give rise

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.120
to many different cell types.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.120
It can also be done in a
fertilized egg, allowing

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.920
the creation of
transgenic animals

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.190
with targeted mutations.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.380
And unlike previous
methods, CRISPR

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.380
can be used to target
many genes at once,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.980
a big advantage for studying
complex human diseases that

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.410
are caused not by
a single mutation,

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.530
but by many genes
acting together.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.260
These methods are being
improved rapidly and will

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.990
have many applications
in basic research,

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.600
in drug development,
in agriculture,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.330
and, perhaps eventually,
for treating human patients

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.230
with genetic disease.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:58.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

